## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** Marker not written.

28 defects remain open (17 critical). No MXFS source changed this session; the work was on the measurement substrate underneath every defect verification.

**Shipped: the SCST fileio bvec use-after-free fix is now the running target, not just a build.** `/sys/kernel/scst_tgt/version` reads `3.11.0-pre+caw-abort-reclaim.2`, `scst_vdisk.ko` srcversion `D621B132576AB508853B189`. The 32-node rig came back clean behind it — 32/32 nodes on 2-path `mpatha` (serial `2e476d07`), 64 sessions, 0 active commands on every production session. sess139 built this and stopped at the relay boundary because installing means dropping the LUN; that teardown/rebuild is now done and the fleet is healthy on the fixed module.

One build-identity trap worth knowing: `scst.ko`'s srcversion is **identical** across `.1` and `.2`. modpost hashes only the `.c` files listed in `.<module>.mod`, so a header-only change doesn't move it. Use `version:`, not `srcversion:`, to tell those two apart.

**Landed a harness correctness fix.** `inflight_ab.sh` resolves its device map via `eval "$(sudo bash stack.sh devmap)"` — and `sudo bash` drops the environment, so `MXFS_FENCE_MODE=fileio` never arrived. A live fileio stack reported "stack not up" while printing blockio device names. The failure mode that matters isn't the nuisance one: an arm could have measured the wrong handler while believing it measured the shipped one. The live stack now records its own mode in `/var/lib/mxfs-fence/mode`, and the precondition dump uses `$TARGET`/`$DM` from devmap instead of hardcoded blockio names.

**What went wrong, and it was mine.** I ran `stack.sh up` a second time on an already-up stack. That reissues `iscsiadm --login`, which reinstates both sessions: `sda`/`sdb` were removed and re-added under new host numbers, the arm's `/dev/sdb` open hit ENXIO mid-setup, and SCST was left holding six sessions on the test target — which on its own breaks validity predicate #1. The departing `sda`'s `sd_remove` sent SYNCHRONIZE CACHE down the dying session, and `stack.sh` sets both `replacement_timeout` and the block-layer timeout to 300s. Result: one kworker stuck in `sd_sync_cache` and four `iscsi_conn_cleanup` threads in `close_conn`. Confined to the fence stack — production stayed at 0 active commands throughout. `fencedelayf`'s `block` attribute reads `0 0`, so this is not the sess37/38 block_count family.

**On whether the UAF is fixed — the honest read is "no evidence yet."** The `loop_unwedge` inspector found the two stranded loop0 requests clean: `0 request(s) with a freed/reused bvec chain`, no GPF anywhere in this session's dmesg. But both are `segs=1`, and under the old bug a ≤4-segment command used the inline `small_bvec` and was never corrupted — sess138's 1-segment request was intact too. That sample sits entirely inside the class that was always clean, so it doesn't test the fix. loop0 also hasn't been recreated since sess136, so those strays may predate the patch.

Next: clear the fence wedge, delete loop0 so stage (ii) starts with no history, bring the fileio stack up exactly once, then run the ruled `0x04 → 0x05 → 0x04` order — the sole closure blocker on D-PR-FENCE-PREEMPT-WITHOUT-ABORT. The >4-segment verification the fix actually needs is the 32-node board itself, now that it runs on a target without the UAF.

